- The conversion of this intersection to All-Way STOP Control involved the following modifications: 
    - Replacing Crossroads Warning signs (W2-1) with STOP Ahead Warning signs (W3-1) on SR 2341 approaches;
    - Installing STOP AHEAD pavement markings and Stop Bars on SR 2341 approaches;
    - Installing oversized (48”) double post-mounted STOP signs (R1-1) on SR 2341 approaches;
    - Replacing existing STOP signs on SR 2340 approaches with oversized (48”) double post-mounted STOP signs (R1-1);
    - Removal of Curve Warning sign assembly on westbound SR 2341 approach.
- This intersection has been on the NCDOT HSIP intersection list from 2019-2023 (#79I00116) and from 2025-2026 (#TSUINT700108)
- Both After Period crasheswere Angle crashes involving northbound vehicles on SR 2340 and westbound vehicles on SR 2341, and fault could not be determined in either instance. The most recent crash took place in June of 2025. 
- The 2 non-Target Crashes in the Before Period were 1 Rear-End crashes involving eastbound vehicles, and 1 Animal crash within the Y-line, to the west of the intersection.

- The conversion of this intersection to All-Way STOP Control involved the following modifications: 
    - Installation of dual oversized STOP signs with Brightsides and stop bars on NC 55 approaches; 
    - Installation of Dual STOP Ahead Warning signs (W3-1) with Brightsides and “800 ft” plaques, and STOP AHEAD pavement markings on NC 55 approaches;
    - Conversion of existing actuated “Vehicles Entering when Flashing” signs on NC 55 approaches to non-actuated flashing STOP Ahead Warning signs (W3-1) and installation of supplemental left-side STOP 
      Ahead Warning signs (W3-1), both with Brightsides and “390 ft” plaques;
    - Installation of STOP Ahead Warning signs (W3-1) with Brightsides on SR 1744 approaches;
    - Replacement of right-side STOP signs on SR 1744 approaches with oversized STOP signs with Brightsides, and installation of Brightsides and Keep Right at Median (R4-7) signs on existing median STOP signs.
- The fatal Target Crash in the Before Period involved a NB vehicle on SR 1744 that failed to stop at the STOP sign and was struck by a WB truck on NC 55, resulting in 5 people being ejected from the NB vehicle. This crash prompted fatal crash investigation slip #200410064CA, which resulted in the recommendation to convert this intersection to All-Way STOP control. This crash was the only crash in both the Before and After Periods that involved the at-fault vehicle running the STOP sign. All other Target Crashes were the result of a failure to yield. 
- There were 2 Target Crashes in the Construction Period, 1 Angle (NB vehicle at fault, struck WB, C-injury, failure to yield), and 1 LTSR (EB vehicle at fault, struck WB, PDO, failure to yield). Both crashes occurred prior to the conversion of the intersection to AWSC, based on the diagrams in the crash reports.

- The conversion of this intersection to All-Way STOP Control involved the following modifications: 
    - Replacement of Crossroads Warning signs (W2-1) with STOP Ahead Warning signs (W3-1) with Brightsides on SR 2406 approaches; 
    - Installation of left-side STOP Ahead Warning sign (W3-1) with Brightside on southbound SR 2406 approach;
    - Installation of  STOP AHEAD and STOP pavement markings on SR 2406 approaches;
    - Installation of dual 36" STOP signs with Brightsides and Stop Bars on SR 2406 approaches;
    - Addition of Brightsides to existing STOP signs on SR 2337 approaches, and installation of new left-side STOP sign on westbound SR 2337 (note: westbound SR 2337 had a median-mounted STOP sign in the 
      Before Period, sign was removed at some point in early 2024, additionally eastbound right-hand STOP sign was changed from oversized (48") to 36" during this project);
    - Installation of STOP pavement markings at Stop Bars on SR 2337 approaches.
- The After Period crash that resulted in A-injuries involved a collision of southbound and westbound vehicles. The reporting officer was unable to determine who was at fault in the collision.
- 8 of the 10 (80%) Before Period Target Crashes involved a southbound vehicle on SR 2406, as did all 3 After Period Target Crashes.
- The most recent After Period crash occurred in November of 2023, and all After Period crashes occurred within 15 months of the AWSC installation. 
- There were 2 Target Crashes reported in the Construction Period, both in June 2022. 1 was a LTSR crash involving 2 northbound vehicles, with 1 attempting to pass the turning vehicle. The other crash was an Angle crash that involved a westbound vehicle on SR 2337 that failed to yield to a southbound vehicle on SR 2406. Both crashes occurred before the intersection was converted to AWSC, and neither resulted in injuries.
